  "notes": "Melodic pop-punk built for radio choruses: 110–186 straight with the backbeat on 2&4, the core singles at 156–180 and the ballads at 81–116, which is where the session floor comes from. Three flat-side majors are named (D-flat, A-flat, E-flat) — \"a capo/tuned-down guitar habit\" — so `keys` is authored. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"straight 8th kick\", \"hat 8ths in verse and crash-8ths in chorus\" (a high open-hat probability with a crash lane), and \"the standard lift is one bar of snare 16ths\", which is the 16th snare-roll ladder at a 4-bar schedule. The bass fact is unusual and is authored as an arrangement device rather than a bass field: it \"drops out for the first two bars of each chorus in some arrangements to make the crash bigger\", which is `dropOutBeats` at 0.92 over 8 beats. The melody \"sits high and thin with the chorus apex on the title's first syllable\", so the register is the highest in this batch, and the \"large gang-vocal stacks\" and \"'whoa-oh' hooks\" are the octave-echo counter at a high ratio. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it medium-high and marks the tempo/key values **[LOW-CONF]** as catalogue readings; `bpm.mode` 168 is this author's centre of the stated core band. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability.",
